WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.859
Baywatch was one of the most successful and
long-running TV shows in history, and it became

00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:11.240
a franchise, with multiple spinoffs, made-for-TV
movies, and even a semi-tongue in cheek feature

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.420
film starring the Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson.

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:18.760
Here's the inside story of one of the biggest
and most universal phenomena in pop culture.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.540
One of the creators of Baywatch, Greg Bonann,
worked as a lifeguard himself patrolling the

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:28.060
beaches of Los Angeles County throughout the
'70s and '80s.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.660
One day he mentioned to fellow lifeguard Michael
Newman that their job might make for good

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:33.240
TV.

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.680
Newman recalled on E!

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.880
True Hollywood Story,

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.300
"[Greg] goes, 'If that show CHiPs could make
it, we could make a show about lifeguarding.'"

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:43.320
Bonann unsuccessfully pitched his show for
10 years.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:47.470
Then in the late '80s, while still working
the beaches, Bonann rescued the children of

00:00:47.470 --> 00:00:52.160
Stu Erwin, an executive at TV production company,
MTM Enterprises.

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.920
Bonann pitched Erwin his series, and while
Erwin passed, he helped the lifeguard refine

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:57.600
his story.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.260
"Looks like we've got a few stubborn die-hards
out there"

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.131
A few lucky connections later, the idea was
developed into a show called Baywatch, by

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:08.640
co-writers Michael Berk and Douglas Schwartz.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.380
It was then pitched to CBS.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:16.300
That network said no, but NBC approved a two-hour
TV movie which would also serve as a pilot

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.020
called Baywatch: Panic at Malibu Pier.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:25.320
Airing in 1989, Panic at Malibu Pier ranked
among the top 10 most popular shows of the

00:01:25.329 --> 00:01:29.850
week, leading NBC to order a Baywatch series
set to debut that fall.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.960
But early Baywatch was less a breezy swimsuit
spectacle and more of an '80s action drama.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.799
It also wasn't that popular, finishing at
Number 60 in the Nielsen ratings, edged out

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.700
by My Two Dads and Father Dowling Mysteries.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.880
NBC's Baywatch run ended after a single season.

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.340
Cast member Billy Warlock told Esquire,

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:52.760
"We didn't get canceled on NBC because of
performance, we just didn't have the money."

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.060
"Let us know if you start to lose it, Mitch!"

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:55.860
"Hah.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:00.020
Watch and learn, grasshopper, watch and learn"

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:05.840
Around that time, European Baywatch distributor
Freemantle asked for another season of episodes,

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.540
claiming that overseas demand for the show
was strong.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:13.140
Co-creator Douglas Schwartz consulted his
uncle, Brady Bunch and Gilligan's Island creator

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.460
Sherwood Schwartz.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:19.520
Having had major success syndicating reruns
of those shows, Sherwood suggested the Baywatch

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:24.159
team buy back the rights to their show, get
a new production company, and enter into the

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.090
lucrative syndicated TV market.

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:27.340
"I'll check the schedule"

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:31.860
By late 1991, Baywatch was ready to go all
over again.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:37.500
As an unproven startup, the first few seasons
of Baywatch had to do things a lot cheaper

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.100
than they had with NBC.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:45.000
Freemantle offered $300,000 an episode, banking
in particular on the show's advertising revenue

00:02:45.010 --> 00:02:49.969
potential in Germany, where David Hasselhoff
was a huge star for both TV and music.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:55.560
U.K.-based TV network ITV threw in some money
too, provided that Baywatch feature no gun

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.880
use and never depict violent acts against
women or children.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:03.360
Still, producers had to cut costs, including
star David Hasselhoff's salary.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.780
"Don't put me in the middle of this"

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.140
Hoff agreed to a 50 percent reduction in his
acting fee in exchange for an executive producer

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:15.560
credit, which made him eligible for a chunk
of the profits should the show find lasting

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:16.280
success.

00:03:16.280 --> 00:03:21.900
According to Fox Business, Hasselhoff wound
up earning about $100 million for his involvement

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:22.900
in Baywatch.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.450
Other, lesser-known stars of the show didn't
make much, however, taking home per-episode

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:30.900
paychecks in the $2,500 to $5,000 range.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:36.680
Obviously Baywatch was popular otherwise it
wouldn't have run for more than 200 episodes

00:03:36.689 --> 00:03:41.610
over more than a decade, launched the careers
of multiple people, and inspired a movie of

00:03:41.610 --> 00:03:42.650
the same name.

00:03:42.650 --> 00:03:48.260
But what is a little surprising, is how staggeringly
popular the show was at the peak of its run.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:54.500
By the mid-'90s, each episode was viewed by
1.1 billion people on average, across more

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:59.020
than 142 countries, and translated into 15
languages.

00:03:59.020 --> 00:04:04.139
With the world population just under 6 billion
at the time, that means roughly one in five

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.000
people worldwide tuned into Baywatch.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:12.040
Broken down into male and female viewers,
one might assume that the swimsuit-clad audience

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:13.840
was overwhelmingly male.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.400
But according to the L.A. Times, about two-thirds
of Baywatch viewers were women.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.240
The reason, according to co-creator Greg Bonann,
is that its female characters were…

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.780
"Positive role models [who were] athletic,
professional, intelligent, and able to make

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.220
any rescue a man can make."

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:30.540
"Hey are you ok?

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:31.400
Is something wrong?

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:35.280
Nothing a vacation wouldn't fix"

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:40.120
Baywatch was never a critically acclaimed
show, but it was fine with its place in the

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.880
TV landscape as a source of eye candy.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:47.820
A near-constant stream of pretty people in
skimpy outfits running on the beach made Baywatch

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.260
memorable as a show slight in content but
heavy on the visuals.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.460
"Yeah, it's a tough job"

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:59.330
Douglas Schwartz helped shape that aesthetic,
both as a co-creator of the series and a director

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:06.220
of 43 episodes and three made-for-TV Baywatch
movies all while qualifying as legally blind.

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:10.260
Diagnosed with extreme "tunnel vision," Schwartz
told the New York Times,

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.780
"My vision is 10 degrees; most people see
180 degrees.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.440
But everything I could see was in-camera."

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.140
According to Schwartz, he's the only legally
blind person to ever be admitted into the

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:23.880
Directors Guild of America.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:30.100
Baywatch made stars out of many up-and-coming
performers, notably Pamela Anderson as lifeguard

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.840
C.J. Parker and a pre-beard, pre-Aquaman Jason
Momoa.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.520
But only a handful of actors could be a part
of the show at any given time, leaving plenty

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:41.180
of famous and talented people off the beach.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:43.380
"Oh my God it's Hulk Hogan"

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:48.900
As the story goes, Scream star Neve Campbell
auditioned but didn't fit in, while future

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:52.500
A-list star Sandra Bullock canceled a scheduled
audition.

00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:57.280
Desperate Housewives star Teri Hatcher was
almost cast, according to co-creator Michael

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:02.411
Berk, while '90s pop singer and dancer Paula
Abdul nearly replaced original cast member

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:07.420
Erika Eleniak, ultimately losing the role
of C.J. Parker to Pamela Anderson.

00:06:07.420 --> 00:06:12.820
David Hasselhoff secured the lead role of
lifeguard chief Mitch Buchannon, but he almost

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:13.580
passed.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:19.060
The former Knight Rider leading man read the
pilot script, and initially rejected the role,

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:23.720
recalling in his memoir, Don't Hassel the
Hoff, that he told his agent it was quote,

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.840
"basically Knight Rider in a bathing suit."

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:30.840
"It's basically Michael Knight running down
the beach in a bathing suit, with beautiful

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:33.300
women, saving lives in the ocean"

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.660
He obviously changed his mind and got the
gig, after producers rejected other '80s TV

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:42.860
stalwarts like Tom Wopat, Lorenzo Lamas, and
William Katt.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.000
Jeremy Jackson portrayed Hobie Buchannon for
nine Baywatch seasons, but he wasn't the producers'

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.000
first choice.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:55.250
He began his stretch at just nine years old,
winding up with the role after another, slightly

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:00.020
older actor had almost landed the part: 15-year-old
Leonardo DiCaprio.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:02.260
Michael Berk told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:04.960
"We actually had DiCaprio ready to be cast."

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.560
So what happened?

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:08.020
Hasselhoff happened.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.500
Hobie is the son of his Baywatch character,
Mitch, and Hasselhoff thought that if a teen

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.810
was portraying his son, it would make him
seem substantially older than if a child were

00:07:16.810 --> 00:07:17.880
to take the part.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:19.220
Douglas Schwartz added,

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.420
"He had a lot of concerns of that type."

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:26.080
But producers ignored Hasselhoff's concerns
when, in season two, they brought on Pamela

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:29.900
Anderson best known for her work on Home Improvement
and in Playboy.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.320
"Oh my God, Mitch, I'm sorry, I was just so
surprised to see you standing there.

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:35.740
Not as surprised as I was"

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.940
Hasselhoff objected to Anderson's casting,
claiming that Baywatch was a family show,

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:43.000
and no place for a Playboy bunny.

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:46.420
He also thought Anderson could be, well, distracting.

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:47.580
Schwartz said,

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:51.740
"She had these enormous breasts and David
thought he would be upstaged by everyone looking

00:07:51.750 --> 00:07:52.750
at her breasts.

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:53.740
Which is what happened."

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:59.580
Apart from hot lifeguards in red swimsuits,
the most memorable images from Baywatch are

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:04.530
its countless slow-motion shots of heroic
lifeguards and women in bikinis running across

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:06.980
the sand to a soft rock soundtrack.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:11.400
By the time that show went into production
in the late '80s, the slo-mo influence of

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:13.820
Chariots of Fire was still being felt.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.840
And apparently, it influenced Greg Bonann,
too.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:18.760
Michael Berk told Esquire,

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:23.520
"Greg had just gotten back from shooting the
Olympics, [namely] sprinters in the 100-yard

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:24.840
dash in slow motion."

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.120
"Alright, come on, get, let's go, huh, show's
over, go back to doing what you were doing"

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:34.180
It was a stylistic choice for sure, but it
was also a practical measure and a cost-saving

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:35.580
technique for producers.

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:37.420
Hasselhoff wrote in his book,

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:42.340
"They'd say to the second unit crew: 'We're
five minutes short go shoot another montage.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:46.240
Grab the most beautiful extra […] and shoot
her running down the beach in slow motion.'"

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:48.020
"Looks good, though, don't you think?"

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.560
Lost isn't the only primarily beach-set show
with a huge cast.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:58.200
Over its 11 seasons and more than 240 episodes,
more than 50 actors were a part of the main

00:08:58.210 --> 00:08:59.580
cast of Baywatch.

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:04.081
That made for one of the biggest casts in
TV history, partially because the show was

00:09:04.081 --> 00:09:05.800
in production for so long.

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:10.480
But there were plenty of other reasons for
the show's revolving door-like personnel changes.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:15.200
Douglas Schwartz claimed that sometimes people
were cast simply because of their agreeable

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.240
appearance, telling Esquire,

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:21.890
"Carmen Electra, Traci Bingham, and Michael
Bergin were all examples of that.

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:25.610
They would look good in a swimsuit, they can
do the action, they could swim.

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:30.140
They just, as actors, were not strong enough,
so we wouldn't give them big storylines."

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:32.120
"Hi.

00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:33.520
I'm Kelly Garrett"

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:39.040
The producer claimed Yasmine Bleeth was, quote,
"doing drugs at the time," and when it started

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:42.120
influencing her work, Baywatch parted ways
with her.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:45.560
Electra quit because she said the show wasn't
a good fit for her.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:50.400
And Pam Anderson eventually walked away, too,
in order to focus on raising her two sons.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:56.180
Baywatch shot primarily on location on real
California beaches with all of their benefits

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:57.140
and risks.

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:03.000
The pilot movie, Baywatch: Panic at Malibu
Pier, filmed in January 1989, when the water

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:08.440
off the coast of Southern California was a
chilly, if not dangerous, 49 degrees.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:13.960
Even worse, according to Hasselhoff, the water
was loaded with garbage, feces, and hypodermic

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:14.860
needles.

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:19.640
Production was halted and moved to Hawaii
when cast members contracted a stomach virus.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:25.100
Still, for the first episode, the ocean wasn't
as clear and refreshing as it looked on TV.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:29.640
According to The Hoff, in order to prevent
cast members from getting extremely sick from

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.740
various toxic elements, he wrote,

00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:35.780
"[We] were doused in disinfectant by medics
every time we came out of the water."

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:41.980
According to Hasselhoff's autobiography, Baywatch
producers saw about 40 hopefuls for the role

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:45.520
of C.J. Parker before eventually hiring Pamela
Anderson.

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:50.060
And unbeknownst to producers, she happened
to be dating cast member David Charvet at

00:10:50.060 --> 00:10:50.760
the time.

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:55.200
"Maybe I should have run into his arms like
a romance novel and kiss him in the sand with

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:57.100
the waves crashing over us."

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:01.900
Pam and David eventually split, and Anderson
eloped with Motley Crüe drummer Tommy Lee

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:03.070
in '95.

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:09.000
Charvet, meanwhile, left the show in '96,
but not before a romantic, onscreen, kiss-filled

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.400
goodbye between his character and hers.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:16.279
That scene filmed on a day when Lee just so
happened to be visiting set and he didn't

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:17.680
handle it very well.

00:11:17.680 --> 00:11:19.700
Douglas Schwartz told Esquire,

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:24.630
"[Tommy Lee] was so upset that Pamela was
kissing David Charvet that he destroyed Pamela's

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:28.500
dressing room kicked in the door, smashed
her mirrors and her windows and things like

00:11:28.500 --> 00:11:29.180
that.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:33.500
We had to have security escort him off the
set and then he was barred from ever coming

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:34.660
on the set again."

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:39.040
In '96, Lee and Anderson's explicit homemade
video leaked to the public.

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:43.660
According to the New York Times, reps for
many outlets that aired Baywatch called for

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:48.940
Anderson's removal from the series, and producers
worried about losing ad revenue, and of potential

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:49.860
cancellation.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:54.860
But the opposite happened: In the aftermath
of the tape, Baywatch scored its highest ratings

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:55.620
ever.

00:11:56.180 --> 00:12:02.780
After nine seasons, Baywatch started getting
stale, so in 1999 it rebooted itself as Baywatch

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:07.220
Hawaii, moving both the setting and filming
to the beautiful island state.

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:12.260
The move was moderately successful, with producers
squeezing two more seasons out of the aging

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:13.040
franchise.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:20.820
"If I'm lucky, if I'm real lucky, I'll find
what I'm lookin' for, I'll find me"

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:25.620
Who knows if it would've lasted even longer
had the original plan to make Baywatch Down

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:27.180
Under not fallen apart.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:32.550
At the time, it was cheaper to film in Australia
than California, and production took advantage

00:12:32.550 --> 00:12:37.600
of the lower costs, filming a two-part episode
in Sydney as a tryout before making a permanent

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:38.180
move.

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:43.340
Local residents strongly objected after members
of the production overstepped their bounds,

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:48.240
allegedly escorting a surfer off the beach
and forcing out noisy skateboarding teens.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:53.140
Area citizens also feared that in the future
they'd have diminished access to the beach,

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:56.540
and they didn't want to deal with the possibility
of looky-loo tourists.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:02.339
Baywatch producers also demanded $200,000
from the Australian government to offset costs,

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:07.120
arguing that the show would ultimately add
$18 million annually to the local economy.

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:10.240
"If you're gonna stay, you better try these.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:12.020
Ah.

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.260
Very nice"

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:19.720
After more than 1,700 residents showed up
for a heated public hearing on the matter,

00:13:19.720 --> 00:13:24.510
the production backed off on its long-term
plans, but only after a contingent from Hawaii

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:25.700
offered a better deal.

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:27.680
Where did that leave Baywatch?

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:34.960
"11 seasons, 245 episodes and um the number
one watched show on the planet"

